title: 'C-c s r : skg-set-relRepo'
pid: 1c364c9f-b546-46cd-9148-1ebcef78c543
body: |2-
  Sets the relRepo of the relationship instance the headline at
  point represents -- =contains= for a content child, the folder's
  relation for a member of an editable partner folder, or the alias membership
  for an alias headline. An unknown viewnode or a restricted viewnode
  in one of those writable positions is supported
  too: it carries only its raw relationship member ID, never a node
  edit. (Formerly
  =skg-privatize-relationship=; renamed because the gesture now moves
  privacy in both directions.)

  For a node-to-node relationship, the command asks the server for its default
  relRepo and current relRepo (the
  "relRepo info" endpoint; the request names the relationship's
  recorder -- the viewparent, or the folder's viewparent -- its member and
  its relation), then prompts -- with both
  tab-completion and S-left/S-right cycling, like the other skgrepo
  dialogs -- over the skgrepos at least as private as the default (more
  public ones could leak an endpoint's ID and would be rejected at
  save), plus a no-override choice. The minibuffer starts pre-filled
  with a pending request when it is offerable, else the current relRepo
  or default, so RET keeps the most specific available choice. If the server cannot resolve the relationship
  (e.g. a node not yet saved), every skgrepo is offered and the save's
  floor check backstops. For an alias, its default is its recorder's home
  and the current relRepo is read directly from the alias metadata, so
  no synthetic graph relationship or broader endpoint is needed.

  For a relationship from an owned recorder to a foreign member, the default is
  the recorder's home regardless of privacy order. That choice deliberately
  shares the foreign ID and relationship with readers of the owned
  skgrepo; Skg never offers the foreign skgrepo as a write destination.

  Choosing a skgrepo writes an =(editRequest (relRepo REPO))= metadata
  request. This
  both raises privacy above the default and lowers it back down to
  (never below) the default -- the way to publicize a relationship stuck at
  an old, more private skgrepo after its endpoint's home skgrepo moved. A relationship
  sitting more private than its default is marked with a red =~NAME= herald
  immediately before the ⌂ skgrepo herald; once saved at its default,
  request and display herald disappear.

  The no-override choice removes the request, which means "no opinion":
  a saved relationship keeps its saved skgrepo (sticky). It does not reset the
  relationship to its default -- to reach the default, choose it.

  Refuses with an error when point is on a member of a write-protected folder
  (hiderFolder, hiddenFolder,
  containerFolder, mentionerFolder, acquiredByFolder, acquiredFolder,
  integratedElsewhereFolder, hiddenInForkPartnerFolder, hiddenByForkerFolder,
  hiddenOutsideOfForkPartnersFolder), or on
  a member of the forksFolder that claims nothing (it forks the
  viewparent, which does not fork it): the relationship belongs to the
  other end there, so setting its skgrepo from this side would be
  meaningless. Also refuses on a root
  headline, which represents no relationship.

  The command only edits the node's metadata in the current buffer.
  Save the view afterward to apply the change.

  Note that the name 'skg-set-relRepo' is somewhat misleading.
  Normally, by 'relationship' we mean
  'a relationship between two nodes in the graph'.
  But for this command we need to broaden the definition to
  'a relationship from what this viewnode represents
  (which might be an alias) to what its parent represents (a node in the graph)'.

  With a prefix argument (=C-u C-c s r=) it instead runs
  'skg-set-relRepo-recursive', described next.
contains:
- 92759638-d534-480d-a259-5f79f851314c
